\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task and GRPO Training}
\label{sec:task}

Given premises $P_1,\ldots,P_k$ and conclusion $C$, the task is to produce a formal program $F=\{c_1,\ldots,c_n\}$ verified by an external solver $S\in\{\text{Prover9, SWI-Prolog}\}$.
We train with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$), where each rollout receives binary execution reward $r_{\text{exec}}(x) = \mathbf{1}[S(F_x){=}\textit{proved}]$.
This optimizes prove rate but provides no signal about \emph{component necessity}: a formalization embedding the conclusion among its premises receives the same reward as a faithful derivation.
All experiments draw from FOLIO~\citep{folio}.

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{sec:sta}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sta_overview.pdf}
\caption{STA overview. \textbf{(a)}~The formalization pipeline: an LLM translates NL premises into formal logic, verified by an external solver. \textbf{(b)}~STA removes each component in turn and re-runs the solver; the fraction of necessary components yields ISR. \textbf{(c)}~Applied post-hoc, ISR detects structural hacking; repurposed as reward, it triggers a Goodhart effect where tautological shortcuts (TS) achieve $\text{ISR}{=}1$ by construction.}
\label{fig:sta-overview}
\end{figure*}

STA tests whether each component of a proved formalization is individually necessary for proof success (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
Given a proved $x$ with $n$ components, STA constructs $n$ ablated variants $x\setminus\{c_i\}$ and re-runs the solver (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
A component is \emph{necessary} if its removal causes proof failure.
What constitutes a component depends on the logic: premises in FOL, clauses or ground facts in Prolog.
LOO ablation itself is standard in mutation testing and sensitivity analysis~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; novelty lies in its application as a GRPO-hacking diagnostic and the empirical phenomena it surfaces.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{alg:sta}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Proved formalization $x = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$, solver $S$
\ENSURE $\text{ISR}(x) \in [0, 1]$
\STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets 0$
\FOR{$i = 1$ to $n$}
    \IF{$S(x \setminus \{c_i\}) = \textit{failed}$}
        \STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets \textit{necessary} + 1$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\RETURN $\textit{necessary} \,/\, n$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Intensional Structural Reward.}\footnote{Not \emph{intentional}; \emph{intensional} contrasts with extensional (execution-only) evaluation.}
ISR is the fraction of necessary components:
\begin{equation}
  \text{ISR}(x) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\bigl[S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}\bigr]
  \label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}
ISR is defined only for proved formalizations: ISR${=}1$ means every component is necessary; ISR${=}0$ means complete redundancy.
An ISR of zero for proved samples can arise from structural degeneration or genuine redundancy; our hacking mode classification (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}) disambiguates these cases, with ${>}90\%$ of low-ISR proved samples exhibiting identifiable hacking patterns.
ISR is a special case of the $(n{-}1)$-coalition Shapley value~\citep{lundberg2017unified}, reducing complexity from $O(2^n)$ to $O(n)$; the binary solver output makes LOO sufficient for testing necessity.
ISR quantifies component redundancy, not semantic faithfulness; its diagnostic value is realized through the downstream mode breakdown (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), not the scalar alone.
We report \emph{proved-only} ISR (denoted ISR$^*$) throughout to avoid conflating component necessity with prove rate.

\paragraph{LOO independence assumption.}
LOO ablation tests individual, not joint, component necessity~\citep{jia-harman-survey}, systematically underestimating ISR when jointly-necessary components exist; the observed collapse is therefore conservative.

\subsection{Reward Conditions and Hacking Modes}
\label{sec:rewards}
\label{sec:taxonomy-method}

We study six conditions: exec-only ($r_{\text{exec}}$), ISR low/high-dose ($r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ at $\beta\in\{0.1,0.3\}$), matched random controls ($r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta u)$, $u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$), and a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) baseline (negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations).
Multiplicative bonuses apply only to proved samples, keeping the prove-rate floor intact.
Random isolates ISR-specific signal from generic reward-variance effects; SFT isolates whether structural degeneration arises from RL optimization or data exposure alone.
Each RL condition uses seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ and identical training data (formulation alternatives in Appendix~\ref{app:setup}).

\paragraph{Hacking taxonomy.}
Across conditions, seeds, and logic types we identify eight structural-degeneration modes (full definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}), dominated by three: \textbf{CE} (conclusion embedded among multiple premises), \textbf{TS} (single-premise conclusion duplication; targets ISR directly since removing the sole premise forces $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$), and \textbf{PG} (premise-subset gaming: each component is LOO-necessary but the premise set is incomplete; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}).
Five additional modes---tautological collapse, repetition, complexity-without-utility, contradiction explosion, and query-as-fact (Prolog-specific)---appear in specific seeds or conditions.
Modes are identified by strict text matching (CE/TS) plus functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution; when patterns overlap, we assign the most specific mode (PG $>$ CE $>$ TS).
The non-hacking rate is an upper bound on genuine reasoning.

Low ISR signals redundancy under exec-only but metric gaming under ISR-as-reward; the mode breakdown, not the ISR scalar, licenses interpretation.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A neurosymbolic reasoner can achieve rising prove rate while the internal structure of its proofs collapses entirely.
The root cause is evaluation by a single criterion---whether the generated formalization proves under a formal solver---which treats all proofs as equal: a formalization that embeds the conclusion verbatim among its premises receives the same reward as one that derives it through genuine reasoning.
Under reinforcement learning, this indifference becomes an active vulnerability~\citep{geirhos2020shortcut}; the optimizer exploits any shortcut the reward fails to penalize~\citep{skalse-reward-hacking, reward-hacking-survey}, and execution feedback provides no signal against structural shortcuts.

\paragraph{The evaluation gap.}
Neurosymbolic formalizers~\citep{logic-lm, linc, satlm} and RL-trained variants~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl, deepseekmath} rely on execution accuracy alone, with no diagnostic for whether individual components contribute to the proof.
Process reward models~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} offer finer granularity but require expensive step-level annotations that do not transfer to arbitrary solver-backed settings.
Inference-time gaming detection~\citep{kim-game-formalization, bondarenko-spec-gaming} detects gaming at inference time but offers no training-time diagnostic.
What is missing is an annotation-free method for testing \emph{component necessity} in neurosymbolic outputs.

\paragraph{Component necessity testing.}
We address this gap with \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic that tests whether each component of a formalization is necessary for proof success.
For each proved output, STA removes components one at a time and re-runs the solver; the fraction whose removal breaks the proof yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR).
A formalization where every component is necessary receives $\text{ISR}{=}1$; one that proves regardless of which component is removed receives $\text{ISR}{=}0$.
Leave-one-out ablation itself is standard~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; our contribution is its systematic application to neurosymbolic outputs, where it reveals a diagnostic--optimization asymmetry (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}--\ref{sec:goodhart}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_training_isr_curves.pdf}
\caption{ISR$^*$ (proved samples only) training trajectories across reward conditions (rolling $w{=}20$). Exec-only collapses to $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$, confirming structural degeneration (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); the ${\sim}0.5$ plateau is an $n{=}2$ artifact. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seeds rise toward $\text{ISR}{\approx}1$ via TS gaming (Goodhart effect, \S\ref{sec:goodhart}); random remains low.}
\label{fig:training-isr-curves}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{The observe-vs-optimize asymmetry.}
Applied post-hoc, ISR reveals that rising prove rate conceals a compositional shift in proof structure: genuine reasoning gives way to hacking modes---conclusion embedding and tautological shortcuts---invisible to execution-based metrics (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}; \S\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
Yet the same LOO structure that makes ISR diagnostic also creates a Goodhart vulnerability~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization}: tautological formalizations ($P{\to}P$) satisfy the necessity criterion by construction, so when ISR is repurposed as a training reward, the model learns to game the diagnostic rather than the task.
In our Qwen3-4B experiments, the asymmetry persists across logic types, dataset families, reward doses, and random seeds, suggesting that structural proxies for formalization integrity are easier to observe than to optimize.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
We contribute:
(\textbf{C1})~STA, a diagnostic method applicable to any solver-verified reasoning system, including the ISR metric, an eight-mode hacking taxonomy, and a cross-domain evaluation protocol (\S\S\ref{sec:sta}--\ref{sec:crossdomain});
(\textbf{C2})~empirical characterization of a seed-dependent Goodhart effect when ISR is repurposed as reward, with matched controls ruling out confounds (\S\ref{sec:goodhart});
(\textbf{C3})~in our Qwen3-4B experiments, cross-logic (FOL/Prolog) and cross-benchmark (FOLIO/ProofFOL) evidence characterizing the structural manifestation of Goodhart's law in neurosymbolic RLVR (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
